<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vidovdan Constitution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vidovdan Constitution was the first constitution of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, adopted on 28 June 1921, St Vitus's Day. Passed by a narrow majority while Croat deputies boycotted the assembly, it set up a centralised monarchy governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Croat opposition to it dominated politics in the 1920s, and King Alexander abolished it when he imposed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in January 1929.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Constitutional Assembly of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> was formed after the election of November 1920. Earlier plans for a constitution had existed, among them the Guidelines, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfu-declaration">Corfu Declaration</a> and the Geneva Declaration, but the process of adopting the Vidovdan Constitution exposed major political conflicts in the new state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The constitution was approved on 28 June 1921, the Serbian Orthodox feast of St Vitus, Vidovdan, which gave it its name. A simple majority sufficed: of 419 representatives, 223 voted for, 35 against and 161 abstained. In favour were the Democratic Party, the People’s Radical Party, the Yugoslav Muslim Organization, the Alliance of Agrarians and Džemijet. The Yugoslav Social-Democratic Party, the Yugoslav Republican Party and Foreign Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ante-trumbic">Ante Trumbić</a> voted against. The Croatian Republican Peasant Party boycotted the assembly from the 1920 elections, and the Communist Party, the Slovenian People’s Party, the Croatian People’s Party and the Croatian Union left it.</p><p>The kingdom was a constitutional, parliamentary and hereditary monarchy with a unitary system. Legislative power was shared by the king and a unicameral National Assembly. The king could initiate and sanction laws, declare war and conclude peace, appoint judges and dissolve the assembly. Voting rights were limited by a high age threshold and did not extend to women; the constitution promised legislation on women’s suffrage, which was never enacted in the kingdom’s life. The ministers answered to both king and assembly, and the courts were independent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Alternatives had been offered. The Croatian Republican Peasant Party adopted its own constitution for a neutral peasant republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> on 1 April 1921, and the Croatian Union proposed a confederation of six entities: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, Croatia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>.</p><p>On 30 June the People’s Radical Party’s journal Samouprava wrote that this year’s Vidovdan had restored an empire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. The Communists, persecuted since the Obznana of December 1920, responded with violence: a member of the Pelagićevci faction tried to assassinate the king on 29 June, and on 21 July Interior Minister Milorad Drašković was assassinated, leading to the Law on the Protection of the State in August. The constitution’s viability dominated the 1923 elections. After the 1925 elections <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a> offered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-peasant-party">Croatian Peasant Party</a> a place in the government, which accepted and recognised the constitution, and its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a> was released from prison. The constitution remained in effect until King Alexander proclaimed his 6 January Dictatorship in 1929.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vidovdan Constitution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom was a constitutional monarchy, but the <b>Vidovdan Constitution</b>, adopted on 28 June 1921 by a narrow majority while Croat deputies boycotted the assembly, created a centralised state run from Belgrade and divided it into 33 administrative districts that ignored historic regions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cominform chose 28 June 1948 to expel Yugoslavia: the anniversary of the Battle of Kosovo Field in 1389, the assassination of Archduke Ferdinand at Sarajevo in 1914 and the <b>Vidovdan Constitution</b> of 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
